=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
A thorough inspection of every relevant component — BGP control plane, physical interfaces,
routing tables, traffic shaping, and firewall rules — reveals no fault. The eBGP session
between router1 (AS 1) and router2 (AS 2) is fully Established and has been continuously
stable since FRR startup, with exactly one connection established and zero drops recorded.
Keepalives are being exchanged at the normal 60-second interval with a 180-second hold
time, well within safe operating margins. Both routers' eth0 interfaces are UP/LOWER_UP
with no link-state changes in the log. Routing tables on both sides are complete: router1
holds a BGP route to 200.1.1.0/24 via 193.10.11.2 and router2 holds 195.11.14.0/24 via
193.10.11.1. End-to-end pings from pc1 to pc2 (200.1.1.2) return 0% packet loss at
sub-millisecond RTTs. No traffic-shaping qdiscs (netem, tbf, htb), no iptables drop rules,
and no rogue processes were found. The only non-standard configuration detail is that both
routers have debug BGP logging directives (debug bgp keepalives, debug bgp updates in/out)
permanently saved in frr.conf; while these are not production best practice, they add only
trivial log overhead and have zero effect on BGP stability or data-plane forwarding. The
helpdesk reporter's premise — that the inter-company link is flapping — is incorrect. The
network is healthy and no corrective action is required. The engineer should advise the user
to look for an application-level or endpoint-side explanation for any perceived drops.
=== END ===
